00238102000700246105001800253106000600271200012200277210007600399215004300475300010000518300002000618327063400638330175801272503005303030606007103083801004303154801000803197930011603205FRBNF435730840000002http://catalogue.bnf.fr/ark:/12148/cb43573084s  a9788495241993bEdiciones el Viso  a8495241994bEdiciones el Viso  a9786076051214  a6076051213brel.  aOCoLC809310979  a20140103d2012    m  y0engy50      ba0 aspa  aMX  aa   b   00|y|  az1 aSurrealismobTexte impriméevasos comunicantesf[coordinación y cuidado editorial Evelyn Useda Miranda... [et al.]  aMéxicocMuseo nacional de arteaMadridcEdiciones el Visodimpr. 2012  a1 vol. (315 p.)cill. en coul.)d31 cm  aCatalog of an exhibition held at the Museo Nacional de Arte, Mexico City, June 5-Sept. 15, 2012  aNotes bibliogr.1 aPresentaciones  ;aIntroducción /Museo Nacional de Arte --aLos vasos comunicantes del surrealismo /Mónica López Velarde Estrada --aDespués del segundo manifiesto, 1929-1933 /Sergio Fauchereau --aEl surrealismo /Didier Ottinger --aMéxico-Paris: El eje surrealista /Philippe Ollé-Laprune --aSurrealistas españoles y surrealismo de España (mapa) /Juan Manuel Bonet --aPintoras surrealistas y el psicoanálisis /Néstor A. Braunstein --aPalabra, mirada y deseo /Helí Morales --aViaje al país de los surrealistas /Fabienne Bradu --aLos días de la calle de Gabino Barreda /Salomon Grimberg --Apéndice  aThe major exhibition "Surrealism: Communicating Vessels", proposes a renewed aesthetic and literary perspective on surrealism, the fundamental artistic movement of the historical avant-gardes of the 20th century, through 120 works and language developed by its most prominent creators, both European and Latin American. The exhibition shows a selection of paintings, sculptures, drawings, photographs and objects from major artists, national or foreign, who developed the principles of surrealism: Salvador Dalí, Raúl Anguiano, Alberto Giacometti, Manuel Álvarez Bravo, Leonora Carrington, among others in Mexico. This carefully edited monograph published as catalogue of this exhibition brings together several essays by renowned specialists and scholars of the movement. Serge Fauchereau, Didier Ottinger and Juan Manuel Bonet approach surrealism from an historical perspective, linked to its origins as a vanguard in Europe, the territory where one of its pillars support the surrealist axis Paris-Mexico, which, Philippe Ollé-Laprune, deals with in turn. The impact of the movement found in Mexico an extremely rich panorama of works created here, as addressed by Fabienne Bradu and Solomon Grimberg. Surrealist imagery, the theory, the appreciation of the intelligence operating in liberty, its relationship with psychoanalysis, and other cultural support elements that structured surrealism are reflected by Mónica López Velarde, Helí Morales y Néstor A. Braunstein. Thus, this monograph is revealed not only as a precise approximation of the art movement from an updated perspective, but as an essential vehicle that travels the communicating vessels that link those territories and moments in which the surrealism shined the most1 aExpositionmMexiconMuseo nacional de artej2012  311933334aSurréalisme311938837xCatalogues d\'exposition2rameau 3aUSbOCoLCc20140103h8093109792marc21 0bBP1  5FR-751131010:43573084001001a2013-190174b759999999cTolbiac - Rez de Jardin - Littérature et art - MagasindO01540cam  2200313   450 001002100000003004700021010003500068020001700103073001800120100004100138101001300179102000700192105001800199106000600217200015200223210006300375215005100438225003800489300003200527410005500559500004100614676001600655686007000671700005000741702006700791801007000858930013900928930015901067FRBNF426497900000005http://catalogue.bnf.fr/ark:/12148/cb42649790h  a978-2-251-33963-4bbr.d27 EUR  aFRb01224336 0a9782251339634  a20120419d2012    m  y0frey50      ba1 afrecgrc  aFR  a|||